  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on context and memory is written, and a short quiz is ready. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or counted its words. I estimate about 4,000 words, which fits the 2,700–6,750 range for a 30-minute lesson.\n\n**The lesson** is `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. It is built around Priya booking the laser cutter on a Wednesday after training on Monday, and covers:\n- **Context window:** what the model sees in one call.\n- **Between steps:** the history is resent on every call, so a long tool result is billed again each time, and a fuller window can lower accuracy (context rot).\n- **Between sessions:** nothing is kept unless your program saves it, for example in a notes file loaded by a tool.\n- **Retrieval:** pulling only the relevant manual passages into the window, either before the call or just in time.\n- **Practice:** a worked example and a \"your turn\" task, plus four checks you can click through for instant feedback.\n\nIts main source is Anthropic's \"Effective context engineering for AI agents\", with Anthropic's context-window docs as the second. All links are ones I opened in this session.\n\n**The quiz** is `quizzes/0002.json`, five new questions with the key in `keys/0002.json`. Ask for it whenever you want to take it.\n\nI added the sources to `RESOURCES.md` and noted the new misconception ids in `NOTES.md`. I did not write a cheat sheet or a learning record.\n\nTwo gaps to know about:\n- I couldn't verify an online community for agent builders, so the lesson sends you to your design review and the makerspace staff instead.\n- Quizzes 0001 (taken twice) is still ungraded. Do you want me to grade it next?",
